  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Advancing the Frontiers of Sustainable Malaria Control in Liberia - A Model for</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Liberia, malaria accounts for 35% of all outpatient visits and 41% of deaths of children under five. With huge investments from the Global Fund and the US President&apos;s Malaria Initiative, rates of malaria in Liberia have dropped since 2005, but are still high: 35% of children under five have malaria parasites in their blood. This Special Initiative will work in two distinct areas. Firstly, it will improve access to malaria diagnosis and treatment by making diagnostic tools and effective malaria medicines available at an affordable price in the private sector in urban slums. In Liberia, artemisinin combination therapy (ACT) is only widely available in public health facilities. But many people in Liberia do not go to these facilities for malaria diagnosis treatment.   There are many reasons for this, but one over-arching factor is the fact that Liberia has recently emerged from a devastating civil war where the health service was all but destroyed. Added to this, the distance to the facility, cost of transport, and concerns about long waiting times and lack of drugs, it&apos;s easy to understand that people often buy malaria medicines from local pharmacies or medicine stores &#x2013; even when they haven&apos;t been tested positive for malaria. In these stores, there are few diagnosis kits and although ACTs may be available, they&apos;re expensive. The result is that poorer people buy lower cost but ineffective malaria drugs. The project will train medicine store staff to correctly diagnose malaria using reliable malaria test kits. Subsidised ACTs will be made available. This new approach, if successful, will influence expansion to other parts of the country and provide lessons for other countries seeking to make diagnosis, and affordable ACTs available through the private sector, ultimately improving access to effective treatment for malaria. Secondly, the project will test the efficacy, acceptability, durability and cost-effectiveness of a new malaria prevention tool called durable wall lining (DL). DL is a material impregnated with insecticides that is affixed to the inside walls of a house. The study will compare DL with another malaria prevention tool, indoor residual spraying. This research project will be one of four studies to assess whether DL could be considered a viable option for large-scale malaria control in Africa.</narrative>
  </description>
